e seventh number (72)**:.\n   - We can use the relationship between LCM and GCD again.\n   - First, find the GCD of 720 and 72:.\n     - First division: \\( 720 \\div 72 = 10 \\) with a remainder of \\( 720 - 72 \\times 10 = 0 \\).\n     - The GCD of 720 and 72 is 72.\n   - Now, use the LCM formula:.\n     \\[.\n     \\text{LCM}(720, 72) = \\frac{|720 \\times 72|}{\\text{GCD}(720, 72)} = \\frac{51840}{72} = 720.\n     \\].\n\n7. **Find the LCM of the result (720) and the eighth number (84)**:.\n   - We can use the relationship between LCM and GCD again.\n   - First, find the GCD of 720 and 84:.\n     - First division: \\( 84 \\div 12 = 7 \\) with a remainder of \\( 84 - 12 \\times 7 = 0 \\).\n     - The GCD of 720 and 84 is 12.\n   - Now, use the LCM formula:.\n     \\[.\n     \\text{LCM}(720, 84) = \\frac{|720 \\times 84|}{\\text{GCD}(720, 84)} = \\frac{60480}{12} = 5040.\n     \\].\n\n8. **Find the LCM of the result (5040) and the ninth number (90)**:.\n   - We can use the relationship between LCM and GCD again.\n   - First, find the GCD of 5040 and 90:.\n     - First division: \\( 90 \\div 12 = 7 \\) with a remainder of \\( 90 - 12 \\times 7 = 6 \\).\n     - Second division: \\( 12 \\div 6 = 2 \\) with a remainder of \\( 12 - 6 \\times 2 = 0 \\).\n     - The GCD of 5040 and 90 is 6.\n   - Now, use the LCM formula:.\n     \\[.\n     \\text{LCM}(5040, 90) = \\frac{|5040 \\times 90|}{\\text{GCD}(5040, 90)} = \\frac{453600}{6} = 75600.\n     \\].\n\n9. **Find the LCM of the result (75600) and the tenth number (108)**:.\n   - We can use the relationship between LCM and GCD again.\n   - First, find the GCD of 75600 and 108:.\n     - First division: \\( 108 \\div 12 = 9 \\) with a remainder of \\( 108 - 12 \\times 9 = 0 \\).\n     - The GCD of 75600 and 108 is 36.\n   - Now, use the LCM formula:.\n     \\[.\n     \\text{LCM}(75600, 108) = \\frac{|75600 \\times 108|}{\\text{GCD}(75600, 108)} = \\frac{8164800}{36} = 227360.\n     \\].\n\n### Summary.\n\nBy understanding t