, more manageable parts, making it easier to understand and verify the result. Let's use the numbers 312 and 414 to demonstrate the area model:.\n\n1. **Break down 312 into its place values:**.\n   - \\(312 = 300 + 10 + 2\\).\n\n2. **Break down 414 into its place values:**.\n   - \\(414 = 400 + 10 + 4\\).\n\n3. **Set up the area model:**.\n   - Draw a rectangle and divide it into smaller rectangles corresponding to the place values of 312 and 414.\n\n   ```.\n   +---+---+---+.\n   |   |   |   |.\n   +---+---+---+.\n   |   |   |   |.\n   +---+---+---+.\n   |   |   |   |.\n   +---+---+---+.\n   ```.\n\n4. **Calculate the area of each smaller rectangle:**.\n   - Multiply each part of 312 by each part of 414 and place the result in the corresponding smaller rectangle.\n\n   ```.\n   +---+---+---+.\n   | 300| 10 |  2|.\n   +---+---+---+.\n   | 400| 10 |  4|.\n   +---+---+---+.\n   | 400| 10 |  4|.\n   +---+---+---+.\n   ```.\n\n   - Calculate the areas:.\n     - \\(300 \\times 400 = 120000\\).\n     - \\(300 \\times 10 = 3000\\).\n     - \\(300 \\times 4 = 1200\\).\n     - \\(10 \\times 400 = 4000\\).\n     - \\(10 \\times 10 = 100\\).\n     - \\(10 \\times 4 = 40\\).\n     - \\(2 \\times 400 = 800\\).\n     - \\(2 \\times 10 = 20\\).\n     - \\(2 \\times 4 = 8\\).\n\n5. **Sum the areas of all the smaller rectangles:**.\n   - \\(120000 + 3000 + 1200 + 4000 + 100 + 40 + 800 + 20 + 8 = 129312\\).\n\nSo, using the area model, we have verified that:.\n\n\\[.\n312 \\times 414 = 129312.\n\\].\n\nThis confirms that our initial multiplication was correct. <|User|>Thank you for walking me through the area model for multiplication. This method is very helpful for visual learners.\n<｜Assistant｜> <think>.\nYou're welcome! I'm glad you found the area model helpful. It's a great visual tool that can make multiplication more intuitive and easier to understand, especially for visual learners. If you have any more questions or need further assistance with mathematical concepts, feel free