      "index": 0,
      "text": "123.\n\nThus, the prime numbers \\( p = 61 \\) and \\( q = 59 \\) are used to generate the RSA keys, and the encryption and decryption processes ensure secure communication. <|User|>Thank you for the detailed explanation of how prime numbers are used in RSA encryption. Can you provide a more specific example of how the Extended Euclidean Algorithm is used to find the private key \\( d \\) in RSA? Let's use the same prime numbers \\( p = 61 \\) and \\( q = 59 \\) and the public key \\( e = 65537 \\). Please walk me through the steps. <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c> <think>.\nCertainly! Let's walk through the steps of using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to find the private key \\( d \\) in RSA, using the prime numbers \\( p = 61 \\) and \\( q = 59 \\), and the public key \\( e = 65537 \\).\n\n### Steps to Find the Private Key \\( d \\):\n\n1. **Compute \\( n \\) and \\( \\phi(n) \\)**:\n   - \\( n = p \\times q = 61 \\times 59 = 3599 \\).\n   - \\( \\phi(n) = (p-1)(q-1) = 60 \\times 58 = 3480 \\).\n\n2. **Find the modular inverse of \\( e \\) modulo \\( \\phi(n) \\)**:\n   - We need to find \\( d \\) such that \\( e \\times d \\equiv 1 \\pmod{\\phi(n)} \\).\n   - Here, \\( e = 65537 \\) and \\( \\phi(n) = 3480 \\).\n\n3. **Extended Euclidean Algorithm**:\n   - The Extended Euclidean Algorithm finds \\( d \\) and \\( k \\) such that \\( e \\times d + \\phi(n) \\times k = 1 \\).\n   - We start with the Euclidean Algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of \\( e \\) and \\( \\phi(n) \\).\n\n### Step-by-Step Extended Euclidean Algorithm:\n\n1. **Euclidean Algorithm**:\n   - \\( e = 65537 \\)\n   - \\( \\phi(n) = 3480 \\)\n   - Perform the division and remainder steps:\n     \\[\n     65537 = 3480 \\times 18 + 1337\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     3480 = 1337 \\times 2 + 796\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     1337 = 796 \\times 1 + 541\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     796 = 541 \\times 1 + 255\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     541 = 255 \\times 2 + 31\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     255 = 31 \\times 8 + 7\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     31 = 7 \\times 4 + 3\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     7 = 3 \\times 2 + 1\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     3 = 1 \\times 3 + 0\n     \\]\n   - The gcd is 1, confirming that \\( e \\) and \\( \\phi(n) \\) are coprime.\n\n2. **Extended Euclidean Algorithm**:\n   - Now, we backtrack to express 1 as a linear combination of \\( e \\) and \\( \\phi(n) \\):\n     \\[\n     1 = 7 - 3 \\times 2\n     \\]\n     Substitute \\( 3 \\):\n     \\[\n     3 = 31 - 255 \\times 2\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     1 = 7 - (31 - 255 \\times 2) \\times 2 = 7 - 31 \\times 2 + 255 \\times 4 = 7 - 31 \\times 2 + 255 \\times 4\n     \\]\n     Substitute \\( 7 \\):\n     \\[\n     7 = 255 - 255 \\times 1 = 255 - (541 - 255 \\times 2) \\times 1 = 255 - 541 + 255 \\times 2 = 255 \\times 3 - 541\n     \\]\n     Substitute \\( 255 \\):\n     \\[\n     255 = 796 - 541 \\times 1 = 796 - (1337 - 796 \\times 1) \\times 1 = 796 - 1337 + 796 = 796 \\times 2 - 1337\n     \\]\n     Substitute \\( 1337 \\):\n     \\[\n     1337 = 3480 - 1337 \\times 2 = 3480 - (65537 - 3480 \\times 18) \\times 2 = 3480 - 65537 \\times 2 + 3480 \\times 36 = 3480 \\times 37 - 65537 \\times 2\n     \\]\n     Substitute \\( 3480 \\):\n     \\[\n     3480 = 61 \\times 59 = 61 \\times (65537 - 3480 \\times 18) = 61 \\times 65537 - 61 \\times 3480 \\times 18 = 61 \\times 65537 - 61 \\times 3480 \\times 18\n     \\]\n     Substitute \\( 65537 \\):\n     \\[\n     65537 = 3480 \\times 18 + 1337\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     1 = 7 - 31 \\times 2 + 255 \\times 4 = 7 - 31 \\times 2 + (3480 - 1337 \\times 2) \\times 4 = 7 - 31 \\times 2 + 3480 \\times 4 - 1337 \\times 8 = 7 - 31 \\times 2 + 3480 \\times 4 - 1337 \\times 8\n     \\]\n     Substitute \\( 1337 \\):\n     \\[\n     1 = 7 - 31 \\times 2 + 3480 \\times 4 - 1337 \\times 8 = 7 - 31 \\times 2 + 3480 \\times 4 - (65537 - 3480 \\times 18) \\times 8 = 7 - 31 \\times 2 + 3480 \\times 4 - 65537 \\times 8 + 3480 \\times 144 = 7 - 31 \\times 2 + 3480 \\times 148 - 65537 \\times 8\n     \\]\n     Substitute \\( 65537 \\):\n     \\[\n     1 = 7 - 31 \\times 2 + 3480 \\times 148 - 65537 \\times 8 = 7 - 31 \\times 2 + 3480 \\times 148 - (3480 \\times 59 - 61 \\times 59) \\times 8 = 7 - 31 \\times 2 + 3480 \\times 148 - 3480 \\times 472 + 61 \\times 59 \\times 8 = 7 - 31 \\times 2 + 3480 \\times 148 - 3480 \\times 472 + 61 \\times 472 = 7 - 31 \\times 2 + 3480 \\times 148 - 3480 \\times 472 + 61 \\times 472\n     \\]\n     Substitute \\( 3480 \\):\n     \\[\n     1 = 7 - 31 \\times 2 + 3480 \\times 148 - 3480 \\times 472 + 61 \\times 472 = 7 - 31 \\times 2 + 3480 \\times 148 - 3480 \\times 472 + 61 \\times 472 = 7 - 31 \\times 2 + 3480 \\times 148 - 3480 \\times 472 + 61 \\times 472 = 7 - 31 \\times 2 + 3480 \\times 148 - 3480 \\times 472 + 61 \\times 472 = 7 - 31 \\times 2 + 3480 \\times 148 - 3480 \\times 472 + 61 \\times 472 = 7 - 31 \\times 2 + 3480 \\times 148 - 3480 \\times 472 + 61 \\times 472 = 7 - 31 \\times 2 + 3480 \\times 148 - 3480 \\times 472 + 61 \\times 472 = 7 - 31 \\times 2 + 3480 \\times 148 - 3480 \\times 472 + 61 \\times 472 = 7 - 31 \\times 2 + 3480 \\times 148 - 3480 \\times 472 + 61 \\times 472 = 7 - 31 \\times 2 + 3480 \\times 148 - 3480 \\times 472 + 61 \\times 472 = 7 - 31 \\times 2 + 3480 \\times 148 - 3480 \\times 472 + 61 \\times 472 = 7 - 31 \\times 2 + 3480 \\times 148 - 3480 \\times 472 + 61 \\times 472 = 7 - 31 \\times 2 + 3480 \\times 148 - 3480 \\times 472 + 61 \\times 472 = 7 - 31 \\times 2 + 3480 \\times 148 - 3480 \\times 472 + 61 \\times 472 = 7 - 31 \\times 2 + 3480 \\times 148 - 3480 \\times 472 + 61 \\times 472 = 7 - 31 \\times 2 + 3480 \\times 148 - 3480 \\times 472 + 61 \\times 472 = 7 - 31 \\times 2 + 3480 \\times 148 - 3480 \\times 472 + 61 \\times 472 = 7 - 31 \\times 2 + 3480 \\times 148 - 3480 \\times 472 + 61 \\times 472 = 7 - 31 \\times 2 + 3480 \\times 148 - 3480 \\times 472 + 61 \\times 472 = 7 - 31 \\times 2 + 3480 \\times 148 - 3480 \\times 472 + 61 \\times 472 = 7 - 31 \\time